{
  "teamId": 139,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Tampa Bay Rays were a team that looked like a championship contender on paper almost immediately — and then spent April proving it would not be easy. Opening the season 13-14, the Rays were bitten by bullpen miscues and timely opponent home runs, yet Joey Wendle's electric three-run blast on Opening Day set the tone for a roster full of unlikely heroes. The early stumbles were real, but they masked a roster that was quietly assembling its pieces.\n\nMay changed everything. The Rays went 22-6 in the most dominant month by any team in baseball that year, a surge fueled by Francisco Mejia's grand slam, consistent starting pitching, and a bullpen that began to lock down late-inning leads. The streak carried into July, when a teenage shortstop named Wander Franco made his debut and instantly delivered, and Austin Meadows came through with the kind of clutch singles that define a pennant-race roster. Brett Phillips, always electric, continued to be a fan favorite with big home runs at key moments. By the end of August, the Rays had built one of the best records in the American League, going 21-6 that month alone.\n\nSeptember brought turbulence. The Rays stumbled to 14-13, their bullpen — particularly Andrew Kittredge — absorbing punishing home runs at the worst times. Yet even in the chaos, Randy Arozarena delivered late-season blasts that kept the team on track for 100 wins, a milestone they reached with their October record. The Rays arrived in the ALDS as one of baseball's elite teams.\n\nThe postseason told a story of flashes and frustration. Jordan Luplow's grand slam in Game 1 briefly lit up Tropicana Field, and Arozarena — who had rewritten postseason history the year before — provided moments of brilliance again. But the Boston Red Sox, armed with Rafael Devers, Christian Vazquez, and a deep lineup, clawed back. Home runs surrendered at critical junctures, including a crushing three-run shot by Devers, ended the Rays' October before it truly began. A 100-win season dissolved in the ALDS, leaving a roster built for October wondering what might have been.",
  "chapters": [
